diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..534aa19
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# Deploy Fizzy to one server with Kamal
+
+The OSS deployment runs Fizzy and its Solid Queue worker in one app container. Kamal Proxy handles HTTPS; SQLite and uploaded files live on the server's disk.
+
+## Before you start
+
+- Point a public DNS name (e.g. `boards.example.org`) at your Linux server. Leave TCP ports 80 and 443 free and open to the internet so the proxy can obtain and renew its Let's Encrypt certificate.
+- Make sure you can SSH to the server and run Docker there. Kamal connects as `root` by default; set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml` if you use another Docker-enabled user.
+- On the machine you deploy **from**, have Docker with Buildx running. In your fork, use the Ruby version in `.ruby-version` and run `bundle install` for `bin/kamal`. The config uses Kamal's local registry over SSH; you don't need a registry account or a public registry port.
+
+## Configure your fork
+
+1. In [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml), replace `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname (no scheme or path). The proxy and Fizzy's email links use this same name. Change `builder.arch` to `arm64` if your server is ARM rather than x86-64.
+2. Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` in the same file. Use a sender your SMTP provider permits. Authenticated mail uses STARTTLS (normally port 587), or implicit TLS on port 465.
+3. Create a private secrets file on the deploy machine:
+
+   ```sh
+   install -m 600 .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   bin/rails secret
+   ```
+
+   Paste the generated value into `SECRET_KEY_BASE` in `.kamal/secrets`. Generate it **once** and keep a secure backup. Set `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` to your mail credentials, or leave both blank for an unauthenticated relay. Single-quote literal values containing `$` or `#`.
+
+   For browser push notifications, generate a VAPID pair and copy both lines into the file:
+
+   ```sh
+   bundle exec ruby -rweb-push -e 'k = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{k.public_key}", "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{k.private_key}"'
+   ```
+
+   Keep these keys across deploys too. Never commit `.kamal/secrets` or put secrets in `deploy.yml`.
+
+## Deploy and update
+
+Commit your non-secret changes (Kamal builds the committed revision of your fork), then run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal config  # Check the target host and volume before connecting.
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Visit `https://boards.example.org` (your hostname) and sign up with your email address. Receiving the verification code confirms that both the background worker and SMTP are working. Use `bin/kamal app logs` to check for errors.
+
+For subsequent releases, commit your changes and run `bin/kamal deploy`. The app prepares the databases on startup and reuses the named Docker volume `fizzy_storage` mounted at `/rails/storage`. It holds the primary, cable, cache and queue databases as well as uploads. Don't remove or rename the volume between releases. A volume survives container replacement, not loss of the server: back it up regularly (e.g. take a snapshot with the app stopped).
